# Task: check that an HTTP endpoint answers successfully within ten seconds.
# Program: this file.
# Run: execute docs/recipes/server-http-health.program --input url=https://example.com/healthz
# Test with fakes: put a fake `curl` earlier on PATH that prints its arguments and exits 0.
#   Expect -fsS, the output discard, the timeout and the URL. Exit 22 to check the failure line.
# Explain: curl's -f makes HTTP error responses a non-zero status.
# Replaces (Bash): curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 10 "$1"
accept url as text
run "curl" with arguments ["-fsS", "-o", "/dev/null", "--max-time", "10", url] and keep status as code
if code is 0
  run "printf" with arguments ["%s is up\n", url]
otherwise
  run "printf" with arguments ["%s is down (curl status %s)\n", url, code]
  run "false"
